module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Away.Basic
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Module.FinitePresentation

/-! # Descent of injectivity and projectivity from principal neighborhoods

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 00EN, 00HT and 00NX.
(blueprint: lem-sc-principal-cokernel-projective-descent).
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false
universe u v w

namespace Module

/-- An injective map with projective cokernel on a principal cover has the same
properties globally, when its source is finite and its target finitely presented.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 00EN (local detection of zero), 00HT (local flatness)
and 00NX (finitely presented flat modules).
(blueprint: lem-sc-principal-cokernel-projective-descent).

Proof: Write Q = N / range f. Its relations are finite: N is finitely presented and
range f is generated by the images of a finite generating set of M. Thus Q is
finitely presented. For a nonzero kernel element x, its annihilator is proper, so
choose a maximal ideal p containing it. The supplied s outside p gives an injective
localized map. Consequently x/1 is zero after inverting s, so some power s^n kills
x. This puts s^n in p, hence s in p, a contradiction. Therefore f is injective.
Tensor product is right exact, so Q localized at s is the quotient of the localized
N by the image of the localized f. This quotient is projective by hypothesis, hence
flat over R_s. Further localization at any prime p in D(s) gives a flat Q_p over
R_p. Every prime admits such s, so local flatness (00HT) makes Q flat over R.
Finally finite presentation and flatness imply projectivity by 00NX. If R is the
zero ring, every unital R-module is zero and both conclusions hold. -/
theorem injective_projective_cokernel_of_principal_cover
    {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    {M : Type v} [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] [Module.Finite R M]
    {N : Type w} [AddCommGroup N] [Module R N] [Module.FinitePresentation R N]
    (f : M →ₗ[R] N)
    (h : ∀ (p : Ideal R) [p.IsPrime], ∃ s : R, s ∉ p ∧
      Function.Injective (f.baseChange (Localization.Away s)) ∧
      Module.Projective (Localization.Away s)
        (TensorProduct R (Localization.Away s) N ⧸
          LinearMap.range (f.baseChange (Localization.Away s)))) :
    Function.Injective f ∧ Module.Projective R (N ⧸ LinearMap.range f) := by sorry

end Module
